From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 02:37:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10988/11171] Record what the model writes once the context
 wall is gone: lookup tables

Two engines from the live r11l run at n_ctx=98304. The truncation is genuinely
fixed -- both are complete parseable Python where the pre-fix induce failed with
missing ('engine',) and chars_final 0 -- and neither induces anything.

Engine 1 is 52 literal coordinate delta strings, memorization in the obvious
form. Engine 2 is the interesting one: zero string literals, a run-length
decoder, tidier code, and a step function that dispatches on three observed
creature positions into three stored blocks of absolute cell assignments. A
fourth position produces nothing.

So the lesson is sharper than "the model memorizes". Between the two attempts it
got better at ENCODING and no better at INDUCING. Compression is not induction
and control flow is not a rule, and a lookup table that reads like a program is
harder to spot than a wall of string literals while being exactly as useless on
an unseen transition.

Engine 2 also states in its own comment that no win state was ever observed, so
is_level_complete cannot be written -- the broken-goal-predicate finding
reproducing live, in the model's words, independent of context size.

This composes with the 2026-08-27 fine-read, which measured the same
memorization class at roughly 6,589-token prompts where no context pressure
existed. Both say the failure lives far below the wall and removing the wall
does not touch it.

Written with its limits in the note rather than around it: n=2, one game, one
model, and neither engine scored. exp6968 in milestone 610 is scheduled to do
the held-out scoring; this is the generation half, and the structural reading is
evidence rather than a measurement.

